  "agent_summary": "2 nutrients are above the upper limit (UL) for a 34-year-old female from these supplements alone. Vitamin D: 175 mcg a day vs a UL of 100 mcg (175%), mostly from Fixture Labs Vitamin D3 5000 IU. The UL guards against high blood calcium (hypercalcemia), which can lead to kidney stones and calcification of soft tissues. Magnesium: 400 mg a day vs a UL of 350 mg (114%), mostly from Fixture Labs Daily Multivitamin. The UL guards against diarrhea and other gastrointestinal upset from supplemental magnesium. Leaving out Fixture Labs Vitamin D3 5000 IU would bring Vitamin D to 50 mcg (50% of the UL). Timing (1 of 2 tips in timing): Fat in the gut enhances vitamin D absorption, so taking it with a meal that contains some fat may help (some is absorbed without fat). Totals exclude food, drinks and medications. This is information, not medical advice; check with a doctor or pharmacist before changing supplements or medications.",
  "assumptions": [
    "dsld:900001: servings_per_day not given; used label maximum of 1 (label: 1 Softgel(s), 1 time(s) daily)",
    "dsld:900002: servings_per_day not given; used label maximum of 2 (label: 1-2 Tablet(s), 1 time(s) daily)",
    "dsld:900003: servings_per_day not given; used label maximum of 1 (label: 2 Capsule(s), 1 time(s) daily)",
    "Totals cover the listed supplements only; food, beverages and medications are not included.",
    "The UL for Iron, Vitamin C, Vitamin D and Zinc applies to total intake from food and supplements; a supplement total under the UL does not show that total intake is under it.",
    "coverage compares supplement totals alone with the RDA (or AI); diet normally supplies much of the reference amount, so low coverage does not indicate a shortfall."
  ],
